{"record_id":"D29-B51-001","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Concurrency","subtopic":"Thread Parking and Notification","concept":"Thread coordination may use explicit parking and notification mechanisms to avoid busy-waiting, but correctness requires a shared predicate or state transition that determines whether progress is actually possible.","knowledge_type":"thread_coordination","language":"Rust","version":"Rust-version-dependent","objective":"Coordinate worker progress without unnecessary CPU consumption.","requirements":["Shared state predicate","Notification mechanism","Defined wake condition","Shutdown state"],"failure_mode":"A thread wakes but immediately sleeps again or waits indefinitely because notification was treated as state rather than a signal to recheck state.","root_cause":"The implementation lacked a predicate-driven wait protocol.","performance_implication":"Proper parking avoids wasteful spinning, while excessive notifications can increase scheduling overhead.","testing":["Worker idle state","State transition","Repeated notifications","Missed progress opportunity","Shutdown"],"verification_requirements":["Wait predicate is explicit","Wake-up always rechecks state","Shutdown can release waiters","No unbounded busy loop exists"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","provenance":"Rust thread synchronization and parking principles.","relationships":["D29-B44-002","D29-B44-005","D29-B51-002"]}
