{"record_id":"D29-B82-001","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"Clone Cost","concept":"Cloning can range from inexpensive value duplication to expensive deep allocation and copying; performance analysis must distinguish the semantic cost of the specific cloned type.","knowledge_type":"performance_concept","language":"Rust","version":"Version-dependent","framework":null,"objective":"Identify materially expensive cloning operations.","requirements":["Type-specific cost analysis","Representative workload","Allocation/copy measurement"],"input":"Rust code containing clone operations","output":"Clone-cost classification","expected_behavior":"Only materially expensive cloning is targeted for optimization.","failure_mode":"Clone calls are removed or replaced with complicated borrowing solely because cloning is assumed to be expensive.","root_cause":"Clone was treated as synonymous with deep allocation.","performance_implication":"Some clones are cheap; others can allocate and copy substantial data.","testing":["Small values","Large values","Repeated clones","Hot path","Concurrent workload"],"verification":["Actual type semantics inspected","Performance claim measured when material","Correctness preserved"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","provenance":"Rust ownership and Clone semantics plus performance-analysis principles.","relationships":["D29-B04-004","D29-B82-002","D29-B85-001"]}
{"record_id":"D29-B82-002","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"Borrow Versus Clone Tradeoff","concept":"Borrowing can avoid ownership duplication but may increase lifetime coupling and restrict mutation or storage patterns; cloning can simplify ownership at measurable memory and CPU cost.","knowledge_type":"tradeoff","language":"Rust","version":"Version-dependent","framework":null,"objective":"Choose between borrowing and cloning using correctness and measured performance requirements.","requirements":["Ownership requirements","Lifetime requirements","Workload measurement","Memory constraints"],"input":"Data-sharing design","output":"Borrow-or-clone decision with rationale","expected_behavior":"The chosen strategy satisfies ownership semantics while meeting measured performance requirements.","failure_mode":"Borrowing introduces complex lifetimes and prevents desired parallelism, or cloning introduces excessive allocation without benefit.","root_cause":"Only one dimension of the ownership/performance tradeoff was considered.","performance_implication":"Borrowing may reduce copies; cloning may improve simplicity and enable independent ownership.","testing":["Small data","Large data","Long-lived data","Concurrent consumers","Mutation"],"verification":["Ownership semantics verified","Performance measured when material","Lifetime complexity reviewed"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":"Rust ownership, borrowing, and performance trade-off principles.","relationships":["D29-B82-001","D29-B79-002"]}
{"record_id":"D29-B82-003","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"String Copy Optimization","concept":"String performance depends on allocation, byte copying, encoding conversion, capacity growth, and ownership requirements; optimizing strings requires measuring the actual workload.","knowledge_type":"performance_pattern","language":"Rust","version":"Version-dependent","framework":null,"objective":"Reduce unnecessary string allocation and copying without weakening correctness.","requirements":["String workload characterization","Encoding assumptions","Ownership model"],"input":"String-heavy Rust operation","output":"Measured string optimization","expected_behavior":"String processing meets required latency and memory objectives while preserving valid UTF-8 semantics where required.","failure_mode":"Repeated temporary strings create avoidable allocations in a hot path.","root_cause":"String conversion and ownership costs were not analyzed.","performance_implication":"Repeated allocation/copying can increase latency and memory traffic.","testing":["Empty strings","Short strings","Large strings","Unicode","Repeated conversion"],"verification":["Allocation/copy behavior measured","Semantic output verified","Optimization regression tested"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","provenance":"Rust String/str ownership and performance principles.","relationships":["D29-B04-002","D29-B81-001","D29-B82-004"]}
{"record_id":"D29-B82-004","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"Reference Counting Cost","concept":"Reference counting provides shared ownership but introduces reference-count operations and synchronization costs depending on the implementation; it should be selected for ownership requirements rather than used as a generic performance optimization.","knowledge_type":"performance_tradeoff","language":"Rust","version":"Version-dependent","framework":null,"objective":"Evaluate shared ownership overhead in performance-sensitive Rust designs.","requirements":["Ownership requirement","Concurrency model","Workload measurement"],"input":"Shared-resource design","output":"Ownership strategy with measured cost","expected_behavior":"Shared ownership is used where required and its overhead is acceptable for the workload.","failure_mode":"Reference counting is introduced broadly where simpler ownership would suffice.","root_cause":"Ownership convenience was confused with performance optimization.","performance_implication":"Reference-count operations add overhead; atomic reference counting can impose additional synchronization cost.","testing":["Single-thread workload","Multi-thread workload","High-frequency clone/drop","Contention"],"verification":["Ownership need established","Relevant workload measured","Alternative designs compared when material"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":"Rust ownership/reference-counting and concurrency performance principles.","relationships":["D29-B10-002","D29-B82-005","D29-B84-004"]}
{"record_id":"D29-B82-005","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"Ownership Cost Model","concept":"Rust ownership operations should be evaluated by their concrete runtime effects—allocation, copying, reference counting, synchronization, cache behavior, and lifetime-driven structure—rather than by ownership syntax alone.","knowledge_type":"performance_reasoning","language":"Rust","version":"Version-dependent","framework":null,"objective":"Provide a reusable model for reasoning about ownership-related performance.","requirements":["Operation-level cost model","Workload context","Measurement where material"],"input":"Ownership design","output":"Performance-aware ownership analysis","expected_behavior":"The analysis identifies concrete cost sources and avoids unsupported claims.","failure_mode":"A design is labeled fast or slow solely because it uses ownership, borrowing, or cloning.","root_cause":"High-level language constructs were used as proxies for runtime performance.","testing":["Representative workload","Hot-path measurement","Memory measurement"],"verification":["Cost claims linked to concrete operations","Execution evidence retained for benchmark claims"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":"Rust ownership semantics and evidence-driven performance analysis.","relationships":["D29-B82-001","D29-B82-002","D29-B82-004"]}

{"record_id":"D29-B83-001","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"Iterator Optimization","concept":"Rust iterators can express composable traversal and transformations while allowing the compiler to optimize abstraction boundaries; performance must still be evaluated for the concrete pipeline and workload.","knowledge_type":"performance_concept","language":"Rust","version":"Version-dependent","framework":null,"objective":"Reason accurately about iterator-based performance without assuming either automatic optimization or overhead.","requirements":["Concrete iterator pipeline","Representative workload","Benchmark or profiling when material"],"input":"Iterator-based computation","output":"Performance characterization","expected_behavior":"The implementation is evaluated based on generated behavior and measured workload.","failure_mode":"Iterator syntax is assumed to be inherently faster or slower than an equivalent loop.","root_cause":"Source-level appearance was treated as proof of runtime behavior.","testing":["Small input","Large input","Empty input","Hot path","Debug/release comparison"],"verification":["Performance claim measured when material","Correctness compared with baseline","Compiler/toolchain context recorded"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","provenance":"Rust iterator abstraction and compiler optimization principles.","relationships":["D29-B06-005","D29-B83-002","D29-B85-002"]}
{"record_id":"D29-B83-002","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"Iterator Allocation Avoidance","concept":"Iterator pipelines can avoid intermediate collections when operations can be composed directly, but materializing a collection can be beneficial when repeated traversal, ownership, or cache locality requires it.","knowledge_type":"optimization_tradeoff","language":"Rust","version":"Version-dependent","framework":null,"objective":"Choose between lazy traversal and materialized collections using workload requirements.","requirements":["Traversal pattern","Ownership requirement","Reuse frequency","Memory constraints"],"input":"Transformation pipeline","output":"Lazy or materialized design","expected_behavior":"The chosen representation minimizes unnecessary work while preserving required semantics.","failure_mode":"Intermediate allocations are removed even though repeated downstream traversal makes materialization cheaper overall.","root_cause":"Allocation avoidance was optimized without considering reuse.","performance_implication":"Lazy processing can reduce temporary allocation; materialization can improve locality and repeated-access cost.","testing":["Single traversal","Repeated traversal","Large input","Memory pressure","Mutation"],"verification":["Allocation behavior measured","Traversal count measured","Correctness preserved"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","provenance":"Rust iterator and collection performance principles.","relationships":["D29-B81-001","D29-B83-001","D29-B83-003"]}
{"record_id":"D29-B83-003","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"Iterator Fusion","concept":"Composable iterator transformations may be optimized into a single traversal, reducing intermediate work; whether such optimization occurs is compiler- and implementation-dependent and should not be claimed without appropriate evidence.","knowledge_type":"compiler_optimization","language":"Rust","version":"Toolchain-dependent","framework":null,"objective":"Capture iterator-fusion reasoning without overstating compiler behavior.","requirements":["Known iterator pipeline","Relevant compiler/toolchain","Performance measurement where material"],"input":"Multiple iterator transformations","output":"Potentially fused traversal","expected_behavior":"The implementation performs required transformations with no unsupported assumption about fusion.","failure_mode":"Performance or allocation behavior is claimed based solely on the number of iterator adapters.","root_cause":"Compiler optimization was assumed instead of verified.","performance_implication":"Fusion can reduce intermediate work, but generated code depends on implementation and optimization context.","testing":["Release benchmark","Debug benchmark","Compiler-version comparison where relevant","Large workload"],"verification":["Claims tied to measured/generated evidence","Toolchain version recorded"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":"Rust compiler optimization and iterator principles.","relationships":["D29-B83-001","D29-B85-003"]}
