{"record_id":"D29-B84-001","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"Cache Locality","concept":"Memory-access locality can materially affect performance because nearby data may be reused efficiently while irregular access patterns can increase cache misses and memory latency.","knowledge_type":"performance_concept","language":"Rust","version":"Target-dependent","objective":"Recognize memory layout and access patterns as performance factors.","requirements":["Workload characterization","Data-layout understanding","Measurement where material"],"input":"Memory-intensive computation","output":"Locality-aware design analysis","expected_behavior":"Data representation and traversal reflect actual access patterns when locality is a material bottleneck.","failure_mode":"A pointer-heavy or scattered representation is used in a hot loop despite poor locality.","root_cause":"Algorithmic and memory-access costs were considered separately when they were coupled.","performance_implication":"Poor locality can reduce throughput and increase latency even when computational complexity is unchanged.","testing":["Sequential access","Random access","Large dataset","Working-set variation"],"verification":["Hot path profiled","Performance measured","Data-layout rationale documented"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":"Systems performance and Rust data-layout principles.","relationships":["D29-B84-002","D29-B85-004"]}
{"record_id":"D29-B84-002","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"Contiguous Data Layout","concept":"Contiguous storage can improve locality for sequential traversal, but layout selection must account for ownership, mutation, element size, access pattern, and algorithmic requirements.","knowledge_type":"data_layout","language":"Rust","version":"Version-dependent","objective":"Select data representations that match access patterns.","requirements":["Access-pattern analysis","Data-size characterization","Mutation requirements"],"input":"Collection design","output":"Layout-aware representation","expected_behavior":"The representation supports efficient access without violating ownership or correctness requirements.","failure_mode":"A theoretically compact representation performs poorly because the workload repeatedly accesses data in a pattern it does not support efficiently.","root_cause":"Layout was selected without workload-level access analysis.","performance_implication":"Contiguous data can improve cache behavior for sequential workloads but is not universally optimal.","testing":["Sequential traversal","Random access","Mutation","Large working set","Small working set"],"verification":["Access pattern documented","Performance measured for material claims","Memory usage evaluated"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","provenance":"Rust collection and cache-locality principles.","relationships":["D29-B81-002","D29-B84-001","D29-B84-003"]}
{"record_id":"D29-B84-003","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"False Sharing","concept":"Concurrent data placed on the same cache line can cause unnecessary coherence traffic when different threads modify independent fields, making memory layout a potential concurrency-performance concern.","knowledge_type":"concurrency_performance","language":"Rust","version":"Target-dependent","objective":"Recognize and investigate cache-line contention in highly concurrent workloads.","requirements":["Concurrent workload","Shared mutable state","Profiling or measurement evidence"],"input":"Multithreaded shared-state design","output":"Contention analysis","expected_behavior":"Independent high-frequency state updates avoid unnecessary cache-coherence contention where it is a measured bottleneck.","failure_mode":"Per-thread counters stored adjacently cause heavy cache-line invalidation.","root_cause":"Data layout ignored cache-coherence behavior.","performance_implication":"False sharing can reduce scalability as thread count increases.","testing":["Single thread","Multiple threads","Increasing thread count","Different data placement"],"verification":["Contention measured","Scaling behavior recorded","Optimization does not alter synchronization semantics"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","provenance":"CPU cache-coherence and concurrent performance principles.","relationships":["D29-B53-004","D29-B84-004"]}
{"record_id":"D29-B84-004","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"Synchronization Contention","concept":"Mutexes, atomic operations, channels, and other synchronization mechanisms can become performance bottlenecks under contention; the correct optimization target depends on the workload and correctness requirements.","knowledge_type":"concurrency_performance","language":"Rust","version":"Version-dependent","objective":"Identify synchronization costs using measured contention rather than removing synchronization unsafely.","requirements":["Correct synchronization contract","Contention measurement","Representative concurrency"],"input":"Concurrent Rust component","output":"Measured synchronization profile","expected_behavior":"Synchronization remains correct while material contention is identified and addressed.","failure_mode":"Locks are removed or weakened to improve timing, introducing a data race or state corruption.","root_cause":"Performance was optimized without preserving the concurrency safety contract.","security_implication":"Unsafe synchronization changes can create memory-safety or integrity vulnerabilities.","performance_implication":"Contention can reduce throughput and increase tail latency.","testing":["Low contention","High contention","Failure paths","Shutdown","Scaling"],"verification":["Concurrency invariants preserved","Contention measured","Performance claim backed by execution evidence"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_HEAVY","provenance":"Rust synchronization and concurrent-performance principles.","relationships":["D29-B10-002","D29-B53-004","D29-B84-003"]}
{"record_id":"D29-B84-005","dataset_id":"D29","topic":"Rust Performance","subtopic":"Memory Footprint Analysis","concept":"Performance optimization should consider peak and retained memory as separate dimensions because reducing latency through caching or reuse can increase memory footprint.","knowledge_type":"memory_performance","language":"Rust","version":"Environment-dependent","objective":"Balance runtime performance against memory constraints.","requirements":["Memory baseline","Peak measurement","Retained-memory analysis","Representative workload"],"input":"Memory-sensitive Rust workload","output":"Latency-throughput-memory tradeoff","expected_behavior":"Optimization remains within declared memory constraints while achieving required performance.","failure_mode":"Caching or allocation reuse improves latency but causes unbounded or unacceptable memory retention.","root_cause":"Memory was not included in the performance objective.","performance_implication":"Higher memory footprint can increase cache pressure, paging risk, or deployment cost.","testing":["Small workload","Large workload","Long-running workload","Repeated workload","Memory pressure"],"verification":["Peak memory measured","Retention measured where relevant","Performance and memory results recorded together"],"validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":"Systems performance and Rust memory-management principles.","relationships":["D29-B81-002","D29-B84-002","D29-B89-005"]}

