{"record_id":"D30-B43-001","dataset_id":"D30","topic":"JVM Memory","subtopic":"Heap Model","concept":"The JVM heap is the runtime memory area from which objects and arrays are allocated, with memory management behavior determined by the selected JVM implementation and garbage collector.","knowledge_type":"runtime_memory","language":"Java","version":"JVM-version-dependent","objective":"Distinguish Java object allocation from application-level ownership and garbage-collection behavior.","requirements":["JVM process","Object allocation","Garbage collector"],"input":"Object allocation","output":"Heap-resident object representation","expected_behavior":"Objects remain reachable while required and become eligible for reclamation when no longer reachable according to GC reachability rules.","failure_mode":"Application assumes that calling a Java method such as close() directly frees an object's heap memory.","root_cause":"Resource lifecycle was confused with garbage-collection lifecycle.","testing":["Allocation workload","Object retention","GC observation"],"verification":["Logical resource lifecycle separated from heap reclamation"],"performance_implication":"Allocation rate and object lifetime influence GC workload and latency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"GENERATED","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","provenance":"JVM heap and garbage-collection principles.","relationships":["D30-B43-002","D30-B44-001","D30-B45-003"]}
